desc(
	mode: "source"
	language: "general"
	title: "Insecure Resteasy Deserialization"
	title_zh: "Insecure Resteasy Deserialization"
	type: vuln
	level: middle
	rule_id: "source-insecure-api-insecure-resteasy-deserialization"
	risk: "deserialization"
	desc: <<<DESC
Migrated from rule `insecure-resteasy-deserialization` (java/jax-rs/security/insecure-resteasy.yaml).
Original message: When a Restful webservice endpoint is configured to use wildcard mediaType {*/*} as a value for the @Consumes annotation, an attacker could abuse the SerializableProvider by sending a HTTP Request with a Content-Type of application/x-java-serialized-object. The body of that request would be processed by the SerializationProvider and could contain a malicious payload, which may lead to arbitrary code execution when calling the $Y.getObject method.
CWE: CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data
References:
- https://access.redhat.com/blogs/766093/posts/3162112
Note: source-mode regex approximation of a safe-literal pattern.
DESC
	solution: <<<SOLUTION
Review this occurrence and replace it with a safer alternative.
SOLUTION
	alert_min: 1
	"file://pos.java": <<<POS
@Consumes({"application/x-java-serialized-object"})
POS
	"safefile://pos.java": <<<NEG
@Consumes({"application/json"})
NEG
)

${*.java}.pattern_regex(/(?:@Consumes\(\{"application\/x\-java\-serialized\-object"\}\))|(?:@Consumes\(\{"\*\/\*"\}\))|(?:@Consumes\("\*\/\*"\))|(?:@Consumes\(\{MediaType\.WILDCARD_TYPE\}\))/) as $hit
alert $hit for {
	level: "middle",
	title: "Insecure Resteasy Deserialization",
	title_zh: "Insecure Resteasy Deserialization",
	message: "Insecure Resteasy Deserialization detected.",
}
